<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shropshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shropshire is a rural county in the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the Welsh border and crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> as its county town. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Cornovii, whose Roman capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum">Viroconium</a> at Wroxeter, and later part of Powys and then Mercia. The Ironbridge Gorge in the east of the county was one of the birthplaces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shropshire.jpg" alt="Shropshire" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Shropshire_Hills_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6083545.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shropshire is a ceremonial county on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a> and Herefordshire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and by Powys and Wrexham in Wales. It covers 3,487 square kilometres, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> as its county town and Telford as its largest settlement. The south-west is upland, taking in the Stiperstones, the Clee Hills, the Long Mynd and Wenlock Edge, and the Wrekin stands isolated west of Telford. The north is a plain, an extension of the Cheshire Plain, where most of the larger towns lie. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> enters from the west, flows through Shrewsbury and leaves the county south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgnorth">Bridgnorth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> occupation, and the hillfort at Old Oswestry dates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. The remains of the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum">Viroconium Cornoviorum</a> survive. After the Roman occupation ended in the 5th century the area lay in the eastern part of the Welsh kingdom of Powys, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> period it was part of Mercia. The county is first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> in the annal for 1006, under the Old English name Scrobbesbyrigscīr, meaning the shire of the fortified place in the scrublands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/much-wenlock">Much Wenlock</a> grew up around the priory founded by the abbess St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mildburh">Milburga</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merewalh">Merewalh</a> of the Magonsæte.</p><p>In the High Middle Ages Shropshire was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welsh-marches">Welsh Marches</a>. From 1472 to 1689 Ludlow was the seat of the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">the Marches</a>, which administered justice in Wales and in Herefordshire, Shropshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>. The border with Wales was fixed in the 16th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the county was Royalist, and Charles II fled through it after his defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, hiding in an oak tree. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/much-wenlock">Much Wenlock</a> was the birthplace of the modern Olympic movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The area around Coalbrookdale in the Ironbridge Gorge is regarded as one of the birthplaces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The first iron-framed building in the world was built at the Flaxmill Maltings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>. The Shropshire Hills form a national landscape. The county has no cities but 22 towns, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswestry">Oswestry</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgnorth">Bridgnorth</a>, Ludlow and Market Drayton. It was called Salop in the official record from 1972 until 1980, when the name Shropshire was restored. For local government it consists of two unitary authority areas, Shropshire and Telford and Wrekin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shropshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early kingdom probably stretched from the Welsh uplands across the upper Severn valley and into the plains of what became <b>Shropshire</b> and Cheshire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Penda of Mercia, allied with the Welsh kingdom of Gwynedd, defeated King Oswald of Northumbria, probably at Oswestry in <b>Shropshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cornovii occupied <b>Shropshire</b>, Cheshire and parts of Staffordshire and the Welsh borderland in the late Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li></ul></section>
